---
import BlogPostLayout from '../../../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro';

const title = "Government psychologist salary in India per month: what each post pays";
const description = "Government psychologist salary in India per month runs from Rs 25,000 on entry contract posts to about Rs 91,000 (basic plus DA) at Level 10. See each post.";
const canonical = "https://futurecareerschool.com/blog/government-jobs/government-psychologist-salary-india-per-month/";
const publishedDateISO = "2026-11-15";
const publishedDateLabel = "15 November 2026";
const authorName = 'Shivanshi Sehgal';
const authorUrl = 'https://futurecareerschool.com/about/shivanshi-sehgal/';
const authorRole = 'Career researcher and writer';

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: "How much does a government psychologist earn per month in India?",
    a: "In the notices checked (2022 to 2026), fixed-amount contract posts for a clinical psychologist paid Rs 25,000 to Rs 50,000 a month. Level 10 posts, such as DRDO Psychology Scientist 'B' and the AIIMS Bathinda child psychologist contract, start at Rs 56,100 basic pay, which is about Rs 91,000 a month once 63% dearness allowance is added and before deductions. A project contract for an assistant professor of clinical psychology at NIMHANS paid Rs 1,30,000."
  },
  {
    q: "Is a government psychologist's salary fixed?",
    a: "A regular post follows a pay level, and the government revises dearness allowance, so the figure moves over time. A fixed-amount contract pays the same sum each month, and the contract runs for a set term, such as 179 days at first at AIIMS Bathinda or one year at a time in Delhi. Read the pay line of the notice before you compare two posts."
  },
  {
    q: "Do I need RCI registration for a government psychologist job?",
    a: "The Kerala PSC clinical psychologist notice asked for it by name, along with an M.Phil in Clinical Psychology or a two-year equivalent from an RCI-approved institute. The AIIMS, NIMHANS and Delhi notices checked asked for the clinical course but did not name registration, so read the exact line in each notice. Using the clinical psychologist title also needs a recognised clinical qualification under the Mental Healthcare Act 2017."
  },
  {
    q: "What is the difference between a clinical psychologist post and a counsellor post?",
    a: "The Delhi Tele MANAS notice paid Rs 50,000 for the clinical psychologist group of posts and Rs 35,000 for a counsellor. The counsellor post accepted a master's in clinical psychology, social work or a related subject, or a bachelor's degree with two years of mental health experience. The clinical posts leaned on the M.Phil or an MA or MSc in psychology."
  },
  {
    q: "Which government psychologist post pays the most?",
    a: "Of the seven notices checked, the highest monthly figure was Rs 1,30,000 consolidated for an assistant professor of clinical psychology on the NIMHANS Tele MANAS apex centre project. It asked for a PhD in Clinical Psychology and 3 years of teaching or research after it. This is not a ranking of every notice in India, because we did not read them all."
  },
  {
    q: "Is there a psychologist job in the armed forces?",
    a: "Yes, through DRDO's Recruitment and Assessment Centre. Its 2022 advertisement listed 20 Psychology Scientist 'B' vacancies, 17 for the Indian Armed Services selection boards and 3 for DRDO. Check rac.gov.in for newer notices."
  },
  {
    q: "Can a government psychologist run a private practice?",
    a: "It depends on the employer, the post and its service rules. Ask for the rule in writing before you plan around it. If outside work is allowed, an RCI-registered clinical psychologist can add a practice or online counselling beside the post."
  },
  {
    q: "Will the 8th Pay Commission change a government psychologist's pay?",
    a: "It may, but not yet. The commission has not reported, and a news report of 14 August 2026 put the end of its 18-month deadline at May 2027. Until then, posts follow the 7th Pay Commission levels, and fixed-amount contract posts only change when a new notice says so."
  },
  {
    q: "Will AI replace government psychologists?",
    a: "Not the regulated clinical post in the near term. Assessment, therapy and the signed report need a registered, accountable person. AI can help with drafting and summaries, but never paste patient details into a public AI tool, and follow your hospital's data rules."
  }
];

const faqJsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  mainEntity: faqItems.map((item) => ({
    "@type": "Question",
    name: item.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: item.a },
  })),
};
---

<BlogPostLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  category="government-jobs"
  publishedAt={publishedDateISO}
  publishedLabel={publishedDateLabel}
  authorName={authorName}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  jsonLdExtras={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <p class="lead-para">
    A government psychologist in India earns about Rs 25,000 a month on a small fixed-amount contract and about Rs 91,000 a month at the first step of a central Level 10 post, counting basic pay plus dearness allowance (DA), a cost-of-living top-up. The gap comes down to one thing: whether the post pays by a government pay level or pays one fixed sum.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Short answer:</strong> In the notices checked (2022 to 2026), fixed-amount contract posts for a clinical psychologist paid Rs 25,000 to Rs 50,000 a month. Level 10 posts, at DRDO and at AIIMS Bathinda, start at Rs 56,100 basic pay before DA.</li>
      <li><strong>How to judge a notice:</strong> "Level 10 plus DA" is a pay level, and "consolidated" is one fixed sum. Then read the qualification line, because clinical posts ask for an RCI-recognised clinical course and some also ask for RCI registration.</li>
      <li><strong>Growth and next step:</strong> A pay level caps what the post alone can pay. A training service and a registered private practice are the two routes that can grow past it, and the high-income skill portfolio you add on the side sets how soon earlier financial freedom comes within reach. Start by reading one live notice end to end and counting its posts.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    Your monthly figure depends on the pay level or fixed sum in the notice, on whether dearness allowance (DA) and house rent allowance (HRA) come on top, and on whether the post is regular or on contract.
  </p>

  <p>
    Every figure below comes from a recruitment notice, or from a news or listing report of one, checked on 16 November 2026. Pay differs with the city, the employer and your skills, and every notice sets its own terms.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you plan to add training or a practice beside a post, the free <a href="/tools/skill-to-service-mapper/">skill-to-service mapper</a> helps you shape a first paid offer.
  </p>

  <p class="more-guides">Deciding whether to wait for a government notice or build your own practice first? <a href="/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/career-counselling-for-government-jobs/">Career counselling for government jobs</a> can help you decide, and the <a href="/blog/government-jobs/">Government Jobs section</a> covers the exam routes.</p>

  <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Article sections">
    <p class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</p>
    <ul class="jump-nav__list">
      <li><a href="#pay">Pay, post by post</a></li>
      <li><a href="#this-week">Do this week</a></li>
      <li><a href="#math">Level 10 to monthly pay</a></li>
      <li><a href="#type">Regular or contract</a></li>
      <li><a href="#qualify">Qualification and registration</a></li>
      <li><a href="#routes">Routes, strongest growth first</a></li>
      <li><a href="#who-pays">Who pays you</a></li>
      <li><a href="#reality">Ceiling, growth and AI</a></li>
      <li><a href="#money">Money check</a></li>
      <li><a href="#checkpoint">Check before you commit</a></li>
      <li><a href="#mistakes">Expensive mistakes</a></li>
      <li><a href="#next-step">What to do next</a></li>
      <li><a href="#sources">Where the figures come from</a></li>
      <li><a href="#faq">FAQs</a></li>
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="pay">Government psychologist salary per month, post by post</h2>
  <p>
    These seven rows are real notices, so each number belongs to one post and one date.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Post and notice</th>
          <th>Monthly pay in the notice</th>
          <th>Type</th>
          <th>What it asked for</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Post and notice"><strong>Psychology Scientist 'B'</strong>, DRDO and armed forces selection boards (RAC advertisement 141, 2022)</td>
          <td data-label="Monthly pay in the notice">Level 10, Rs 56,100 pay matrix entry. The notice quoted about Rs 88,000 a month with HRA and allowances at metro rates</td>
          <td data-label="Type">Regular. 20 posts: 17 for the armed forces selection boards, 3 for DRDO</td>
          <td data-label="What it asked for">At least a First Class master's in Psychology, plus a valid GATE (Psychology, XH) score or NET qualification for Assistant Professor</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Post and notice"><strong>Child psychologist</strong>, AIIMS Bathinda (walk-in, April 2025)</td>
          <td data-label="Monthly pay in the notice">Level 10: Rs 56,100 basic pay plus DA. No other allowances</td>
          <td data-label="Type">Contract. 1 post, 179 days at first, extendable to two years at most or until a regular appointment</td>
          <td data-label="What it asked for">MA or MSc Psychology with M.Phil in Clinical Psychology, and at least 2 years in child and adolescent mental health</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Post and notice"><strong>Clinical Psychologist</strong>, Health Services, Kerala PSC (category 428/2025)</td>
          <td data-label="Monthly pay in the notice">Pay scale Rs 55,200 to Rs 1,15,300</td>
          <td data-label="Type">State direct recruitment. 1 vacancy, for SIUC Nadar community candidates</td>
          <td data-label="What it asked for">M.Phil in Clinical Psychology or an equivalent two-year course from an RCI-approved university, plus RCI registration as Clinical Psychologist</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Post and notice"><strong>Clinical Psychologist</strong>, Tele MANAS, Delhi State Health Society (last date 17 August 2024)</td>
          <td data-label="Monthly pay in the notice">Rs 50,000. The counsellor post (20 posts) paid Rs 35,000</td>
          <td data-label="Type">Annual contract. 4 posts shared by clinical psychologists, psychiatric social workers and psychiatric nurses</td>
          <td data-label="What it asked for">A First or Second Class M.Phil in Clinical Psychology, or an MA or MSc in Psychology, listed as alternatives</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Post and notice"><strong>Clinical Psychologist</strong>, Karnataka Brain Health Initiative at NIMHANS (17 January 2024)</td>
          <td data-label="Monthly pay in the notice">Rs 40,000 consolidated</td>
          <td data-label="Type">Project contract. 32 posts</td>
          <td data-label="What it asked for">M.Phil in Clinical Psychology. If none is available, an MA or MSc in Psychology could be considered</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Post and notice"><strong>Clinical Psychologist or Psychologist</strong>, NHM Karnataka mental health staff (notice of 2 September 2026, a listing site's summary)</td>
          <td data-label="Monthly pay in the notice">Rs 25,000. A March 2026 Udupi district listing showed Rs 26,250 for a clinical psychologist</td>
          <td data-label="Type">Contract</td>
          <td data-label="What it asked for">A postgraduate psychology degree from a regular course, not by correspondence. We could not open the official PDF, so confirm it there</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Post and notice"><strong>Assistant Professor or Senior Consultant, Clinical Psychology</strong>, NIMHANS apex centre for Tele MANAS (19 April 2024)</td>
          <td data-label="Monthly pay in the notice">Rs 1,30,000 consolidated. A Senior Consultant with under 3 years of experience was to get Rs 1,05,000 if no suitable candidate applied</td>
          <td data-label="Type">Project contract. 1 post, a first appointment of 6 months, 5% increment each year</td>
          <td data-label="What it asked for">MA or MSc Psychology, RCI-recognised M.Phil in Clinical Psychology and PhD in Clinical Psychology, plus 3 years of teaching or research after the PhD</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="note-line">
    "Consolidated" means one fixed sum with no extra allowances. A Level 10 post is paid as basic pay plus DA, and a regular post may add HRA and other allowances.
  </p>

  <h2 id="this-week">Do this week</h2>
  <p>Read one live notice end to end this week. It teaches more than any pay summary.</p>
  <div class="week-box">
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Day 1.</strong> Open one live notice for a psychologist post. Write whether it pays a pay level or one consolidated sum, whether it is regular or contract, and what qualification it asks for.</li>
      <li><strong>Day 2.</strong> Check with your institute that your course is recognised by the RCI (Rehabilitation Council of India), and whether the post asks for RCI registration.</li>
      <li><strong>Day 3.</strong> Turn the pay into a monthly figure with the worked Level 10 example below. Add house rent allowance only after you read what the notice says for your city.</li>
      <li><strong>Day 4 to 7.</strong> List three employers from this page, such as AIIMS and DRDO, and check their careers pages once a week for new posts. Write each date you check.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="week-box__note">A contract post can pay much less than a regular post and may end with the project. Ask about renewal before you decide.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="math">How a Level 10 pay level becomes a monthly figure</h2>
  <p>
    Level 10 starts at Rs 56,100 basic pay in the 7th Pay Commission matrix. The Union Cabinet raised central DA from 60% to 63% of basic pay from 1 July 2026, a decision reported on 28 October 2026.
  </p>
  <div class="ladder">
    <div class="ladder-step">
      <div class="ladder-step__num">01</div>
      <div>
        <div class="ladder-step__title">Basic pay: Rs 56,100</div>
        <p>The first step of Level 10, as written in the DRDO and AIIMS Bathinda notices.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="ladder-step">
      <div class="ladder-step__num">02</div>
      <div>
        <div class="ladder-step__title">DA at 63%: Rs 35,343</div>
        <p>DA is a share of basic pay, and the government revises it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="ladder-step">
      <div class="ladder-step__num">03</div>
      <div>
        <div class="ladder-step__title">Basic plus DA: Rs 91,443</div>
        <p>Pay before HRA, allowances and deductions at the first step of Level 10.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="ladder-step">
      <div class="ladder-step__num">04</div>
      <div>
        <div class="ladder-step__title">Then add and subtract</div>
        <p>On a regular post, HRA and transport allowance depend on the city. Pension contribution, tax and other deductions come off.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>
    This is arithmetic from the published pay level and DA rate, not a pay slip. The 2022 DRDO notice quoted about Rs 88,000 with HRA at metro rates, when DA was lower.
  </p>
  <p>
    The 8th Pay Commission is still at work and has not changed these levels. A report of 14 August 2026 put the end of its 18-month deadline at May 2027, so treat every figure as today's picture and not a promise.
  </p>

  <h2 id="type">Regular post or contract post: what changes in your monthly pay</h2>
  <div class="split-grid">
    <div class="split-card split-card--grow">
      <div class="split-card__label">Regular post</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Pay comes from a pay level or pay scale, and a regular post can carry DA, HRA and other allowances.</li>
        <li>Selection is by exam and interview. DRDO weighted the subject exam 80% and the interview 20%.</li>
        <li>Postings can be anywhere. The DRDO notice said selected candidates must serve anywhere in India, including remote and field areas.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="split-card split-card--risk">
      <div class="split-card__label">Contract or project post</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Pay is usually one fixed sum. AIIMS Bathinda was the exception: Level 10 basic pay plus DA, with no other allowances.</li>
        <li>The term is short. AIIMS Bathinda started at 179 days and allowed two years at most, and Delhi's Tele MANAS contracts ran year to year after a performance review.</li>
        <li>Most contract notices do not promise a regular post, so plan your next step before the term ends.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="qualify">Qualification and registration behind each post</h2>
  <p>
    The pay line is half of a notice. The qualification line decides whether you can apply at all.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Gate</th>
          <th>What it means</th>
          <th>Where it appeared</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Gate"><strong>Master's in Psychology</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What it means">The base degree. DRDO asked for at least a First Class</td>
          <td data-label="Where it appeared">Every post in the table</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Gate"><strong>RCI-recognised clinical course</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What it means">M.Phil in Clinical Psychology, or the two-year equivalent that is replacing it</td>
          <td data-label="Where it appeared">AIIMS Bathinda, Kerala PSC, NIMHANS and Delhi notices</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Gate"><strong>RCI registration</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What it means">Registration as Clinical Psychologist with the Rehabilitation Council of India</td>
          <td data-label="Where it appeared">Kerala PSC notice</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Gate"><strong>GATE (XH) or NET</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What it means">A valid GATE score in Psychology, or NET qualification for Assistant Professor in Psychology</td>
          <td data-label="Where it appeared">DRDO Psychology Scientist 'B'</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Gate"><strong>PhD plus experience</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What it means">Years of teaching or research after the doctorate</td>
          <td data-label="Where it appeared">NIMHANS assistant professor post</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Under the Mental Healthcare Act 2017, a clinical psychologist needs a recognised qualification in clinical psychology, or a postgraduate degree in psychology plus a two-year M.Phil with supervised clinical training. A general MA or MSc in psychology does not give the title by itself. Counsellor posts, such as Delhi's at Rs 35,000, are a separate role, so use only the title your qualification allows.
  </p>
  <p>
    The RCI kept the M.Phil running for the 2024-25 and 2025-26 sessions. NIMHANS now lists its two-year course for the 2026 intake as a Master's in Clinical Psychology (RCI), formerly the M.Phil, and the Kerala notice already accepts an equivalent two-year course. If a notice names only the M.Phil, ask the recruiter in writing whether the new master's counts.
  </p>
  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      You face two filters, not one. The first is a seat in the clinical course: NIMHANS listed 32 seats for its 2026 intake, 22 of them all-India. The second is a post: the 2022 DRDO notice had 20 psychology vacancies for the whole country, and the Kerala PSC notice had one, reserved for a single community.
    </p>
    <p>
      These numbers cannot give you honest selection odds. Count the posts in the last few notices before you plan a year around one.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="routes">Routes for a psychologist who wants government pay, strongest growth first</h2>
  <p>
    Routes are ranked by how far your own work can lift income past a pay level, strongest first. The tags are our reading of how each route is built, not measured data. Among the three government routes, the order runs from steadiest pay to shortest term.
  </p>
  <div class="route-grid">
    <div class="route-card">
      <div class="route-card__top">
        <span class="route-card__n">01</span>
        <span class="chip chip--high">Scalable</span>
      </div>
      <h3>Training, workshops and assessment services for schools, colleges and companies</h3>
      <p class="route-card__why">One programme can be sold again and again, so income is not capped by your own session hours.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>Who pays you</span>Schools, colleges, employers and NGOs that book your programmes.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--violet"><span>What earns trust</span>Your degree, a clear outline and feedback from the first group.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--gold"><span>Watch out</span>Stay inside your qualification. Clinical work needs RCI registration.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>How it grows</span>Turn one workshop into a fixed package, then train someone else to deliver it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="route-card">
      <div class="route-card__top">
        <span class="route-card__n">02</span>
        <span class="chip chip--mid">Scalable with a business layer</span>
      </div>
      <h3>Private practice or online counselling after RCI registration</h3>
      <p class="route-card__why">You set the fee and the schedule, and can add group programmes and supervision later.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>Who pays you</span>Clients and their families.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--violet"><span>What earns trust</span>Registration, supervised experience and referrals from doctors and schools.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--gold"><span>Watch out</span>The first clients come slowly, and a government post may restrict outside work.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>How it grows</span>Group programmes, supervision of juniors and institute contracts.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="route-card">
      <div class="route-card__top">
        <span class="route-card__n">03</span>
        <span class="chip chip--low">Not so scalable</span>
      </div>
      <h3>Regular central post: DRDO and armed forces selection boards, and Level 10 hospital posts</h3>
      <p class="route-card__why">Stable pay and DA, but a fixed ladder and very few posts.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>Who pays you</span>The government, through the pay matrix.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--violet"><span>What earns trust</span>Marks, a GATE or NET score, an interview and the exact qualification asked.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--gold"><span>Watch out</span>Postings can be remote, and notices are irregular.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>How it grows</span>Promotions by service rules, then a practice or training work beside or after service.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="route-card">
      <div class="route-card__top">
        <span class="route-card__n">04</span>
        <span class="chip chip--low">Not so scalable</span>
      </div>
      <h3>State health service post through a public service commission</h3>
      <p class="route-card__why">A state pay scale and a state job, with one or a few vacancies per notice.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>Who pays you</span>The state government.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--violet"><span>What earns trust</span>The RCI-recognised course, RCI registration and your exam rank.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--gold"><span>Watch out</span>Notices are rare, and some are reserved for set communities or categories.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>How it grows</span>Step up through the state's promotion rules, or move to a larger hospital.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="route-card">
      <div class="route-card__top">
        <span class="route-card__n">05</span>
        <span class="chip chip--low">Not so scalable</span>
      </div>
      <h3>Contract posts under health missions and projects</h3>
      <p class="route-card__why">The easiest door in, but the term is short and, in most notices, the pay is one fixed sum.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>Who pays you</span>A state health society, a mission or a funded project.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--violet"><span>What earns trust</span>The course the notice asks for and a record of supervised cases.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--gold"><span>Watch out</span>Fixed-amount clinical psychologist posts in the notices checked paid Rs 25,000 to Rs 50,000 a month, with no promise of a regular post.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>How it grows</span>Use the term to build supervised experience, then move to a pay-scale post or your own practice.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <strong>The ownership path, in the order above:</strong> a training service scales first, because one programme can be packaged and handed to a trainer. An RCI-registered private practice comes next, because you set the fee and can add group programmes and juniors. Check your employer's outside-work rule before you start either.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="who-pays">Who pays a government psychologist, and how they judge you</h2>
  <p>
    Your pay comes from the public purse: a selection board, a state health society, a government hospital or a funded project such as Tele MANAS. Each checks the papers its notice names, which here means the clinical course, RCI registration where asked, a GATE or NET score or an exam rank, and years in a named setting such as child and adolescent mental health.
  </p>
  <p>
    Start with one skill: a clear assessment report, written from supervised cases that a senior can sign off. Selling counts mostly on the side, or after you leave the post: pick one group to serve, such as school students or caregivers, keep one anonymised outcome note, and ask the doctors and teachers who already trust you for referrals. Watch how many referrals turn into a first paid session.
  </p>

  <h2 id="reality">Pay ceiling, growth and AI for a government psychologist</h2>
  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Your pay rises with the pay level and the promotion rules, not with the number of people you help. The daily work is assessments, therapy, case records and written reports, and on a selection board it is testing candidates and accepting postings that can be remote.
    </p>
    <p>
      Salary alone makes financial freedom slow, because the pay level caps what the post can pay. A steady salary still lets you save and invest, and outside-work rules differ by employer, so ask for yours in writing.
    </p>
  </div>
  <p>
    AI helps most with paperwork, such as turning your own notes into a first draft of a report and summarising papers. Assessment, therapy and the signed report stay with a registered, accountable person, so the risk to regulated clinical posts is low for now.
  </p>
  <p>
    The pressure is on generic wellness chat and entry-level counselling that needs no registration, where software can already do part of the job. This is our judgement, with no statistic behind it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Staged path: for now, learn to draft and summarise with AI from anonymised notes and inside your hospital's data rules. Later, deepen what software cannot do: assessment judgement, supervision, and explaining results to anxious families.
  </p>
  <p>
    Beside the post, build the skill mix that makes you hard to replace: one core skill (clinical assessment), one multiplier (training design or data handling), clear English and the local language for families, and proof others can check. These are the decisions that unlock high income opportunities, whatever the pay level says.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
